WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
If you're moving from traditional iOS development to React Native,

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.000
the relational mechanics you've used to build interfaces

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.000
disappear the moment you start writing code.

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Auto Layout is a system of equations.

5
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
You declare that a views leading edge is 16 points from its superview,

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.000
or that its width equals 50% of its container.

7
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.000
At render time, a constraint solver resolves every relationship

8
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.000
to calculate the final frames.

9
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.000
React Native replaces the solver with yoga,

10
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.000
a flexbox engine built on directional flow rather than relational equations.

11
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Each container dictates how immediate children arrange along a main axis

12
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.000
and distribute space.

13
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.000
It governs only direct children.

14
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Crossview dependencies do not exist.

15
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.000
React Native layout applies the same declarative logic found in Swift UI stacks.

16
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000
You are mapping your existing mental model of containers and modifiers

17
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.000
directly to the yoga engine.

18
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.000
The mechanic at work here is that containers own the layout of their direct children.

19
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
This shift in responsibility is the core concept required

20
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
to transition from coordinate-based constraints to a flow-based architecture.

21
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
While React Native uses web CSS vocabulary,

22
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
it modifies foundational defaults to suit mobile rendering.

23
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
On the web, flex direction defaults to a row.

24
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.000
In React Native, it defaults to column.

25
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
A standard view stacks children vertically,

26
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.000
exactly like a Swift UI v stack.

27
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Yoga also diverges from the web regarding compression.

28
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.000
In browser-based flexbox, elements can shrink to fit their containers by default.

29
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.000
In React Native, the flex shrink property defaults to zero.

30
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.000
With a flex shrink of zero,

31
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.000
child components maintain their intrinsic size,

32
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.000
even when the container runs out of space.

33
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.000
This behavior aligns with the rendering expectations found in native iOS development.

34
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Engineers who assume yoga behaves exactly like browser-based flexbox,

35
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
often encounter unexpected layout results.

36
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Identifying these mobile first defaults makes the behavior of the layout engine predictable from the start.

37
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.000
A settings row features an icon,

38
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.000
stabbed labels, and a chevron.

39
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.000
SwiftUI uses an h stack with a nested v stack and spacer.

40
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
In React Native, the outer container is a view with flex direction row.

41
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.000
The inner view defaults to column, stacking the text vertically.

42
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.000
SwiftUI's stack spacing parameter,

43
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
which applies uniform distance between children,

44
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.000
is handled in React Native at a gap property.

45
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.000
SwiftUI's spacer consumes available space to separate neighbors.

46
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.000
React Native does this two ways.

47
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
First, applying flex one to a child container gives it all remaining space,

48
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.000
pushing the chevron out.

49
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Alternatively, justify content space between on the parent

50
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.000
pins the outer elements to the edges.

51
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.000
For cross-axis alignment, SwiftUI's stack alignment maps to align items on the parent.

52
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
If you need a single child to break from the group,

53
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.000
you use a line self on that specific child.

54
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Combining these flow directions with space distribution

55
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.000
allows you to recreate native list geometries

56
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.000
without manually calculating the position of a single element.

57
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.000
While stacks handle the X and Y axes,

58
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.000
layering along the Z axis, like a status badge on a profile picture,

59
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.000
requires different tools.

60
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.000
SwiftUI uses a Z stack or . overlay.

61
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.000
Yoga lacks a Z stack.

62
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Instead, setting position absolute removes a child

63
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.000
from the standard layout flow.

64
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
An absolute child determines its coordinates relative to its nearest ancestor

65
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.000
that is also positioned absolute or relative.

66
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.000
This is the same mechanic used when placing a UI view

67
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.000
with translates auto resizing constraints set to false

68
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.000
without providing any constraints.

69
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Mastering this flow removal mechanism

70
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.000
lets you build custom navigation bars,

71
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.000
badges and floating action buttons

72
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.000
without affecting the layout of the primary content containers.

73
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
For static sizing, SwiftUI's frame modifiers

74
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.000
map to width and height in points.

75
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.000
.frame maxwith.infinity equals a width of 100%.

76
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Note the difference.

77
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Percentage width is an absolute calculation of the parent

78
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.000
while Flex1 takes a share of remaining space.

79
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.000
Adaptive layout sometimes require a child

80
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.000
to know the exact dimensions of its environment.

81
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.000
In SwiftUI, this is the role of the geometry reader.

82
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.000
React Native provides two solutions.

83
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.000
The dimensions API allows you to fetch the static boundaries

84
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.000
of the hardware screen or the application window.

85
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.000
For the size of a specific component,

86
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.000
you use the on layout prop.

87
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.000
This callback triggers once the view has been measured

88
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.000
and positioned by the engine, providing exact width,

89
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.000
height and local coordinates.

90
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Using on layout provides the dimensional data needed

91
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.000
to build interfaces that respond to their own rendered size,

92
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.000
enabling complex logic that static modifiers cannot support.

93
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.000
We can synthesize these rules by looking at a complex content card,

94
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.000
featuring a cover image, an absolute overlay and a wrapped tag cloud.

95
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.000
The root container uses the default column flow

96
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.000
to stack the sections.

97
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Inside, FlexRap forces overflowing tags to the next line,

98
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.000
a native FlexBox behavior.

99
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.000
Below, the action row uses FlexDirectionRow and GAP

100
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:20.000
for consistent button spacing without manual margins.

101
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.000
This architecture is governed by three principles.

102
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.000
Containers own their children, directional flow replaces

103
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.000
relational equations, and optimized for vertical stacks.

104
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.000
The React Native layout engine is a predictable flow model.

105
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.000
By translating your experience with SwiftUI stacks

106
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.000
modifiers into yoga properties, you can build native quality interfaces

107
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.000
using the logic you already understand.

